{
  "dom_id": "step-q47541",
  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14194326/assets/9c1312d8d745f65bbcd3e2756abcbc4f8f5a7058904efea1c8c9b1d43edeec70.png)\n\nThe share price, in dollars, of a rapidly growing company after $t$ years is given by the function $R(t),$ whose graph is shown above. \n\nApproximate the rate at which the share price is increasing when $t=2$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$",
  "worked_solution": "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{R(t)-R(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2.$  Applying the above formula with $t=2$ and $h=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{R(2)-R(2-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{R(2)-R(1)}{1} \\\\  & =7\\,000-1\\,000 \\\\  & =6\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the rate of increase is $$6\\,000\\,/\\text{year}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
